4-in-a-row (**I7-H8-G9-F10**) forces Black to play **E11**. However, this is just a "check" that Black can easily answer. Crucially, it leaves the **H7** spot open. On the next turn, Black would play H7, creating an Open 3 and taking the initiative, likely leading to a loss for White.\n3.  **E10:** This blocks the other side of Black\'s diagonal. However, Black can still play **H7** to form a 3-in-a-row. While blocked on one side (at E10), it allows Black to maintain pressure. H7 is a much more active blocking move for White.\n\n**Reason:**\nWhite must prioritize defense against Black\'s potential Open 3 at **H7**. Playing at **H7** not only neutralizes this threat completely but also simultaneously develops White\'s own attack by forming a vertical line of 3 stones and connecting adjacent clusters. It is a dual-purpose move that seizes the initiative.\n\nAnswer:\n```json\n{\n  "col": "H",\n  "row": 7\n}\n```'}
